formatted_question:
According to the paper "The Era of 1-bit LLMs: All Large Language Models are in 1.58 Bits", the name of the 1-bit LLM variant introduced by the authors in which every single parameter of the LLM is ternary $\{-1, 0, 1\}$ is
(A) \textbf{\text{LLaMA 2 7B}}
(B) \textbf{\text{BitNet b1.58}}
(C) \textbf{\text{BitNet 1.58B}}
(D) \textbf{\text{BitNet}}
(E) \textbf{\text{BitNet b1}}

According to the paper "The Era of 1-bit LLMs: All Large Language Models are in 1.58 Bits", the significant 1-bit LLM variant introduced by the authors, where every parameter is ternary and takes values in $\{-1, 0, 1\}$, is called
(A) \textbf{\text{BitNet}}
(B) \textbf{\text{BitNet b1}}
(C) \textbf{\text{AWQ}}
(D) \textbf{\text{QLoRA}}
(E) \textbf{\text{BitNet b1.58}}

raw_knowledge_statement:
It first scales the weight matrix by its average absolute value, and then round each value to the nearest integer among \{-1, 0, +1\}:

\begin{equation}
    \widetilde{W} = \mathrm{RoundClip}(\frac{W}{\gamma + \epsilon}, -1, 1),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{RoundClip}(x, a, b) = \max(a, \min(b, \mathrm{round}(x))),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \gamma = \frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} |W_{ij}|.
\end{equation}

formatted_question:
According to the paper "The Era of 1-bit LLMs: All Large Language Models are in 1.58 Bits", in the absmean quantization function used to constrain the BitLinear weight matrix to \{-1, 0, +1\}, \gamma is defined as
(A) \frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} W_{ij}
(B) \frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} |W_{ij}|
(C) \sqrt{\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} (W_{ij}-\bar{W})^{2}}
(D) \sqrt{\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} W_{ij}^{2}}
(E) \max_{ij} |W_{ij}|

raw_knowledge_statement:
It first scales the weight matrix by its average absolute value, and then round each value to the nearest integer among \{-1, 0, +1\}:

\begin{equation}
    \widetilde{W} = \mathrm{RoundClip}(\frac{W}{\gamma + \epsilon}, -1, 1),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{RoundClip}(x, a, b) = \max(a, \min(b, \mathrm{round}(x))),
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \gamma = \frac{1}{nm}\sum_{ij} |W_{ij}|.
\end{equation}

